		 <td>                            
	                                   

<P>By the time I arrived, Master Sureshot was groggy with loss of blood. The bladed arrowhead had severed an artery in his leg and the ground around the rock was slippery with blood. For a moment, I stood in shock, in disbelief that a man could bleed so much.</P><br><P>The howling war-cries of the gnolls on the hill above us woke me from my shock.</P><br><P>"Kartum, your belt!" </P><br><P>Kartum was pressed against the rock, his eyes wide with fear. His bow had dropped useless to the ground. </P><br><P>"Kartum! Pick up your bow and give me your belt! I need to bind Master Sureshot’s wounds!"</P><br><P>The other initiate, a young half-elf named Cheskin, cursed, snatching Kartum’s bow up off the ground and pressing it into the shivering woodsman’s hands. He then whipped off his belt and helped me to bind Master Sureshot’s leg. </P><br><P>Our Master had lost much blood. My hands, tightening the tourniquet were shaking with fear, but Master Sureshot opened his eyes and looked into mine. It was not the gaze of a dying man I saw there, but the resolve of a hunter. His eyes were unafraid.</P><br><I><P>N</I>o, I thought to myself, <I>We will not die here. Not here. Not today</I>.</P><br><P>Cheskin and I quickly wrapped him in our cloaks, shivering form the cold and the fearful howling of the gnolls.</P><br><P>We had all but forgotten about Kartum. He was huddled in a ball beneath the rock. The fear had taken his mind.</P><br><P>Cheskin looked at me and nocked an arrow to his string, "How many are there, do you think?"</P><br><P>I shook my head, "I saw no tracks, but the arrows…three, perhaps four."</P><br><P>Cheskin put his back to the rock and tried to crane his neck around to have a look up the hill. An arrow sparked off the stone next to his face and he jumped back. </P><br><P>He blew out a breath, "Have you ever fought a gnoll before?"</P><br><P>"No…I have not." I laughed, the situation was suddenly funny to me. "In fact, I have never drawn blade, nor bow in anger against….anything. Have you?"</P><br><P>Cheskin loosened his sword in the scabbard, "I once got punched in the face by the bouncer at Fish’s. He is large and hairy, but that is as close as I have come. What should we do?"</P><br><P>I thought for a moment, surveying the slope of the hill. "We can’t wait. Even if we could out-shoot them, they would go and get their friends if the fight went agianst them. Runing is out. Master Sureshot will die if we leave him."</P><br><P>"Then we fight."</P><br><P>"Aye…" I was thinking fast. They could take us simply by coming down both sides of the rock at the same time.</P><br><P>The howling continued, growing closer, and all about me I fancied I heard the snapping of twigs. We had to act fast. "Give me your quiver."</P><br><P>"Aye." Cheskin handed the quiver over.</P><br><P>I turned to Kartum, huddled against the rock, "Kartum, snap out of it, we need you!"</P><br><P>In response, he rocked slighly back and forth fetally on his hanuches and shivered, looking up at me.</P><br><P>Dropping to one knee I grabbed him by the tunic and shook him,"Dammit, Kartum we need you! You are the best shot among us now! We need your skill! If we are to live, we need your skill <B><I>now</B></I>! Cheskin is going to run, and draw their fire…"</P><br><P>"Err…I am?"</P><br><P>"…while you shoot them. I will protect your back when they come for you."</P><br><P>It was true. I was the strongest in close combat, being much bigger than either of them. Kartum was the best shot, and Cheskin was the fleetest of foot.</P><br><P>Somehow, Kartum composed himself, wiped the tears from his eyes and gripped his bow.</P><br><P>I handed Cheskin’s quiver to Kartum.</P><br><P>Cheskin unsheathed his blade and glared at Kartum. "Shoot straight, please."</P><br><P>I leaned my bow and qiver against the rock and bared my sword. "Go Ches!"</P><br><P>…And he was off.</P><br>         <br>
